---
/**
 * The privacy page, and the only one.
 *
 * It lives at /privacy/ rather than under /docs/ because that is the address a
 * store listing, a repository and this site's own header already point at, and
 * it is listed in the documentation navigation like any other page. It covers
 * the app and this website, because splitting them produced two pages that
 * each said half of the same thing.
 *
 * Every claim here is checked against the app's source by tools/verify-facts.mjs.
 * If you change a sentence about behaviour, change the check with it.
 */
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../components/docs/Callout.astro';
import { page } from '../data/docs-nav';
import { GITHUB_URL } from '../data/site';

const entry = page('/privacy/');
---

<DocsLayout page={entry}>
  <h2>The one sentence that matters</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Nothing leaves your iPhone until you add a destination and confirm it.</strong> There is
    no default destination, no hosted relay, and no server belonging to the maintainer.
  </p>

  <h2>What leaves, and when</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Only to destinations you configured.</strong> Each destination can be limited to
      specific Health types, so it receives what you scoped it to and nothing else.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Only when its cadence says so</strong>, or when you tap Sync now, or when a Shortcut
      runs. A destination set to manual sends nothing on its own.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A connection test carries no Health data.</strong> It is a fixed probe in the
      destination's own format — for JSON, literally
      <code>{'{"kind":"hozzConnectionTest","schemaVersion":1}'}</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Opening the destination picker looks for receivers on your local network.</strong>
      Hozz browses for Bonjour services and, failing that, probes addresses on your own subnet. This
      is discovery traffic on your network only; it carries no Health data and reaches nothing
      outside it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Nothing else makes a network request.</strong> No analytics endpoint, no crash
      reporter, no update check, no feature flags — so there is no request to inspect and find
      innocuous.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Credentials</h2>

  <p>
    Destination secrets — bearer tokens, API keys, MQTT passwords — are held in the device Keychain
    as <code>kSecAttrAccessibleAfterFirstUnlockThisDeviceOnly</code>. That accessibility is what lets
    a background sync read them after the phone has been unlocked once since boot, and it also means
    the item never syncs and is not carried in a backup. A test in the app asserts that constant
    directly.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>The Mac receiver's pairing token is the deliberate exception</strong>, and it is worth
    being exact about the mechanism. The token is stored with
    <code>kSecAttrSynchronizable</code> set, so it syncs through <em>your own</em> iCloud Keychain,{' '}
    <em>and</em> it is confined to a shared Keychain access group that only Hozz's apps can claim.
    Those are two different protections doing two different jobs: the first lets a phone and a Mac
    signed into the same Apple ID find each other without you copying a token by hand, the second
    stops any other app from reading it. If iCloud is unavailable or the devices are on different
    Apple IDs, nothing is shared and the phone pairs over the local network instead.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="note" title="One thing that is not a secret">
    <p>
      An MQTT <strong>username</strong> is stored with the rest of a destination's settings, not in
      the Keychain — only the password is. That matches how MQTT is normally treated, but it means
      the username is readable in the app's own database rather than protected as a secret.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>What is stored on the phone</h2>

  <p>
    Read positions, per-type coverage state, destination configuration, delivery receipts and staged
    data waiting to be sent. <strong
      >Hozz does not keep a permanent local mirror of your Health history</strong
    > — it reads from Health, stages what it is about to send, and lets that go.
  </p>

  <p>
    That database is written with <code>completeUnlessOpen</code> file protection and
    <strong>excluded from device backups</strong>. The protection is applied to each file rather than
    only to the folder, because SQLite creates its <code>-wal</code> and <code>-shm</code> companions
    after the folder already exists and they would otherwise be left out.
  </p>

  <h2>What is stored on the Mac</h2>

  <p>
    The Mac receiver keeps what the phone sends it in a SQLite database in its own application
    support folder, and that database <strong>is</strong> a durable copy of your Health records —
    that is the point of it. It is excluded from backups.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="warn" title="macOS has no equivalent of iOS file protection">
    <p>
      There is no per-file encryption class on the Mac tied to a passcode, so the receiver's database
      is protected by FileVault or it is not protected at rest at all. Turning FileVault on is your
      decision and Hozz cannot make it for you.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>What never reaches a log</h2>

  <p>
    Hozz logs through Apple's unified logging system: status events and failure descriptions. <strong
      >No Health sample value, credential or destination secret appears in any log call</strong
    >, and tests exist to fail if one ever does. There is no in-app log viewer and no way for the app
    to collect or send a log — the only way to read one is through Console on your own machine.
  </p>

  <p>
    One exception worth naming: when the Mac publishes itself for pairing it logs its own local
    network addresses. Those are addresses on your network, not credentials and not Health data.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="note" title="Why response bodies are thrown away">
    <p>
      When an endpoint rejects a batch it frequently echoes the offending record back. So Hozz
      records the status code and a human-readable failure state and <strong
        >discards the body</strong
      > — which is mildly inconvenient when debugging your own server, and the correct trade when the
      body may contain a Health value.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Health access</h2>

  <p>
    Hozz asks Health for <strong>read access only</strong>; it never requests permission to write,
    and it never writes back. Access is requested when you first export or sync, not at launch.
    <a href="/docs/data-coverage/#what-hozz-will-never-do"
      >Why it will never write back is on Data coverage.</a
    >
  </p>

  <h2>What Hozz cannot promise</h2>

  <p>
    Once data reaches a destination, that copy is yours to look after, and Hozz has no further say.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A server you point it at holds your Health data.</strong> If it is on the public
      internet, it should be behind TLS and authentication, and its operator — probably you — decides
      what happens next.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>An assistant connected to <a href="/docs/mcp/">the MCP server</a> receives real
        readings.</strong> The server runs locally and is read-only, but one of its tools returns
      individual samples — timestamp, value, unit, source — to whatever assistant asked. If that
      assistant is cloud-hosted, those readings go to its provider. That is the assistant's
      behaviour, and no local read-only server can prevent it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A synced folder goes wherever that folder syncs.</strong> Choosing iCloud Drive or
      Dropbox means choosing their handling of it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Choosing destinations you actually control is the whole discipline this app is built around, and
    it is the one part it cannot do for you.
  </p>

  <h2>This website</h2>

  <p>
    Static files on Cloudflare Pages. No analytics script, no tag manager, no advertising pixel, no
    A/B testing, no cookie. Typefaces are served from this domain rather than a font CDN, so loading
    a page tells no third party you were here; the page loads no third-party resources at all.
    Cloudflare keeps operational logs the way any host does — that is outside my control and covered
    by Cloudflare's own policy. Following a link to GitHub puts you on GitHub's site under GitHub's
    policies.
  </p>

  <h2>How to check any of this</h2>

  <p>
    Everything above is a negative claim, and a negative claim cannot be demonstrated by using the
    app — only by reading it. Hozz is GPL-3.0 and developed in the open, and the behaviour described
    here is meant to be proven by tests rather than by this page.
    <a href={GITHUB_URL} rel="noopener">Read the source.</a>
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
